    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 2. Content standards: 1.2.1.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| name the parts of an audio-visual text |  |  |\n| take notes from a documentary or news broadcast |  |  |\n| record figures and attribute them to a source |  |  |\n| tell what was said from what the pictures suggested |  |  |\n| recognise persuasive technique in a broadcast |  |  |\n| detect bias in what a programme includes and leaves out |  |  |\n| initiate, build on and probe in a collaborative discussion |  |  |\n| summarise a broadcast for someone who did not see it |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory, before you open the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** What is a documentary?\n\n**2.** What is a voice-over?\n\n**3.** What is a caption?\n\n**4.** What is a sound bite?\n\n**5.** Name two things a listener should always write down\n\n**6.** What is bias?\n\n**7.** What is an interviewee?\n\n**8.** Give one probing question\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A text that carries meaning through sound and pictures together is an … text\n\n**2.** The filmed material used in a programme is\n\n**3.** The spoken explanation accompanying pictures is the\n\n**4.** Naming the person or body a figure came from is\n\n**5.** Starting a new line of discussion is\n\n**6.** Adding to what another speaker has said is … on\n\n**7.** A question that presses for evidence or explanation is a … question\n\n**8.** Write, in your own words, why what a documentary chooses to film is part of its argument.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Guided practice — Note-taking in four columns**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\n- **Facts and figures** — write the number and the ______ it came from.\n\n- **Who said it** — name and role; a nurse and a minister are not the same ______ .\n\n- **What the pictures showed** — separately from what was said.\n\n- **My questions** — anything unexplained. This column is the most ______ afterwards. Notes taken in one column become a paragraph nobody can use.\n\n> **Guided practice — Finding the selection**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.* List every kind of person who ______ in the programme. Then list the kinds of person the subject involves who did not appear. A programme about school fees with no ______ in it has made a choice. Ask what each absent voice would have ______ . **Selection is not the same as lying.** Every fact may be true and the picture still be one-sided.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Name six parts of an audio-visual text and say what each contributes.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Watch or listen to a broadcast. Take notes in the four columns.\n\n_[answer space — 6 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write down three figures from the broadcast, each with its source.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** Write two things the pictures suggested that the commentary did not say.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** Name four persuasive techniques a broadcast can use.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**6.** List the kinds of people who appeared in the broadcast, and two kinds who did not.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**7.** Write one probing question you would put to the programme's makers.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**8.** Distinguish fact from opinion in three statements taken from the broadcast.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**9.** Summarise the broadcast in not more than eighty words for someone who did not see it. Give your count.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**10.** Say what a listener loses by watching a broadcast without taking notes.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** Compare two reports of the same event from different sources. Set out three differences and say what each suggests about the source.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n**2.** Explain how a programme can be entirely accurate and still be biased.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write a two-minute script for a documentary sequence on a local subject, with commentary, one caption and one sound bite.\n\n_[answer space — 9 lines]_\n\n**4.** Take a broadcast and rewrite one sequence to favour the other side, changing nothing that is false.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n**5.** Chair a fifteen-minute discussion on the broadcast and record how many times each member initiated, built on and probed.\n\n_[answer space — 6 lines]_\n\n\n\n> **Writing task**\n\nWatch or listen to a documentary or news report of at least five minutes. Write a review of about two hundred and fifty words: what it was about, four facts with their sources, two things the pictures said that the words did not, the persuasive techniques used, whose voices were included and whose were left out, and one question the programme did not answer. **Plan first**\n\n| **The programme and its subject** | **Four facts and where each came from** |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| **Techniques I noticed, and where** | **Voices present and voices absent** |\n|  |  |\n\n**Now write.**\n\n_[answer space — 18 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**Check your work**\n\n| **I have…** | **✓** |\n| --- | --- |\n| attributed every figure to a named source |  |\n| separated what was said from what was shown |  |\n| named at least two persuasive techniques with examples |  |\n| named a voice the programme left out |  |\n| ended with a question the programme did not answer |  |\n\n> **Speaking and listening**\n\n*This task is carried out aloud. Your teacher or a partner signs it when you have done it.* Report a broadcast to a group who did not see it, in ninety seconds. They must be able to state its central claim and one piece of its evidence afterwards.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\nHold a fifteen-minute group discussion on the broadcast in which every member initiates once, builds on another's point twice, and asks one probing question.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Name six parts of an audio-visual text and say what each contributes. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**2.** Name the four columns of a listening note and say what goes in each. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Explain what attribution is and why a figure without one is of little use. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** Name four persuasive techniques used in broadcasts. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Explain how a programme can be accurate in every statement and still be biased. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**6.** Explain the difference between initiating, building on and probing in a discussion. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Writing task | 10 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Oral Language (Listening and Speaking) · Sub-Strand: English Sounds*"
