    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 7. Content standards: 3.1.1.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| tell a phrase from a clause |  |  |\n| find the head word of a noun phrase |  |  |\n| use pre-modifiers and post-modifiers |  |  |\n| keep adjectives in the accepted order |  |  |\n| use quantifiers with countable and uncountable nouns |  |  |\n| use phrasal verbs and place the particle correctly |  |  |\n| use adverbial phrases of manner, place, time and frequency |  |  |\n| front an adverbial for emphasis |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory, before you open the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** What is a phrase?\n\n**2.** What is the head word of *the tall boy in blue*?\n\n**3.** Is *rice* countable or uncountable?\n\n**4.** Which is correct: *many rice* or *much rice*?\n\n**5.** Give a phrasal verb meaning *to postpone*\n\n**6.** Put in order: *(red / a / large) box*\n\n**7.** Find the adverbial: *She spoke very quietly.*\n\n**8.** What does *fronting* mean?\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A group of words working as a unit but containing no subject and verb is a\n\n**2.** The word a phrase is built around is the … word\n\n**3.** A word standing before the head and describing it is a\n\n**4.** A phrase standing after the head and describing it is a\n\n**5.** A word such as *much*, *many* or *several* that tells how much or how many is a\n\n**6.** The small word attached to a phrasal verb, such as *up* or *off*, is the\n\n**7.** A phrasal verb whose object may come between verb and particle is\n\n**8.** Write, in your own words, the difference between a phrase and a clause.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Guided practice — Building a noun phrase**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.* Start with the head: *______* . Add a determiner: *the officer*. Add pre-modifiers, in order: *the tall young ______* . Add a post-modifier: *the tall young officer ______ the gate*. **The whole phrase is one noun.** Test it: replace it with *he* and the sentence still works.\n\n> **Guided practice — Placing the particle**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\n**Separable:** *turn off the light* or *turn the light ______* — both correct. **But with a pronoun**, only one is possible: *turn it off*, never *turn off ______* .\n\n**Inseparable:** *look after the child* — never *look the child ______* . Which of these is separable: *put off, call on, give up, run into*? ______ **Learn them in sentences.** No rule tells you which is which.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Say whether each is a phrase or a clause: (a) *in the blue overall* (b) *when the bell rang* (c) *running very fast* (d) *she waited*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Underline the head word: *the tall man in the blue overall; a bag of red beans; three of my closest friends*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Expand *woman* into a noun phrase with a determiner, two pre-modifiers and a post-modifier.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Put in order: *(cotton / white / beautiful / a) dress*; *(metal / round / old / an) tank*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Choose the quantifier: (a) *___ rice* (b) *___ pupils* (c) *___ information* (d) *___ chairs*. Use *much*, *many*, *few*, *little*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** Correct: *He gave me many advices. There were less pupils today. I have a few money.*\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**7.** Give the meaning of each phrasal verb: *put off, call on, give up, run into, look after, bring up*.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**8.** Rewrite with the pronoun in the right place: *turn off it; look the child after; put off it*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**9.** Name the kind of adverbial: (a) *with great care* (b) *at the roadside* (c) *every Friday* (d) *before the rains*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**10.** Front the adverbial and say what changes: *The lorry arrived at dawn, slowly and without lights.*\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** Write one sentence containing a noun phrase of at least eight words, and label every part.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**2.** Explain why *fewer* and *less* are not interchangeable, and give four correct examples.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**3.** Take ten phrasal verbs and sort them into separable and inseparable, giving a test sentence for each.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n\n\n**4.** Write the same sentence four times, fronting a different adverbial each time, and say what each version emphasises.\n\n_[answer space — 7 lines]_\n\n**5.** Explain why *The boy in the blue shirt who won the prize was absent* is one noun phrase plus a verb, and mark its parts.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n> **Writing task**\n\nWrite a description of a place or an event of about two hundred and fifty words. It must contain four noun phrases of at least six words each, six phrasal verbs of which two are inseparable, four adverbial phrases covering manner, place, time and frequency, and two fronted adverbials. Underline the noun phrases and circle the phrasal verbs. **Plan first**\n\n| **My subject** | **Four long noun phrases** |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| **Six phrasal verbs, two inseparable** | **Where the two fronted adverbials go** |\n|  |  |\n\n**Now write.**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 21 lines]_\n\n**Check your work**\n\n| **I have…** | **✓** |\n| --- | --- |\n| underlined four noun phrases of at least six words |  |\n| used six phrasal verbs and placed every pronoun correctly |  |\n| used adverbials of manner, place, time and frequency |  |\n| fronted two adverbials for emphasis |  |\n| kept adjective strings to three before any one noun |  |\n\n> **Speaking and listening**\n\n*This task is carried out aloud. Your teacher or a partner signs it when you have done it.* Describe a person to the class in one long noun phrase without naming them, adding modifiers until they are identified. The class says which modifier gave it away.\n\n\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\nIn pairs, hold a two-minute conversation in which each speaker must use six phrasal verbs naturally. Your partner lists them and marks any that sounded forced.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Explain the difference between a phrase and a clause, with an example of each. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**2.** Underline the head word: *the tall man in the blue overall*; *a bag of red beans*. **[2]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Put in order: *(cotton / white / beautiful / a) dress*. **[2]**\n\n**4.** Correct: *He gave me many advices. There were less pupils today. I have a few money.* **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** Give the meaning of *put off, call on, give up, look after*, and say which is inseparable. **[5]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n**6.** Front the adverbial and say what changes: *The lorry arrived at dawn, slowly and without lights.* **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Writing task | 10 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage · Sub-Strand: Grammar*"
