    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 13. Content standards: 4.2.2.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| write a formal letter with section headings |  |  |\n| use a subject line and a copy line correctly |  |  |\n| write the minutes of a meeting |  |  |\n| set out an agenda |  |  |\n| write a letter to the editor |  |  |\n| design a flyer or poster that a reader can use |  |  |\n| write a speech for an occasion |  |  |\n| use persuasive devices honestly |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory, before you open the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** Where does the writer's address go in a formal letter?\n\n**2.** Which ending goes with *Dear Sir*?\n\n**3.** What does CC mean on a letter?\n\n**4.** What are minutes?\n\n**5.** What is *any other business*?\n\n**6.** Name two things a poster must have\n\n**7.** What is an action point?\n\n**8.** Name one persuasive device used in speeches\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A heading dividing a long letter into parts is a … heading\n\n**2.** The line naming the business of a letter or email is the subject\n\n**3.** The list of items a meeting will take, in order, is the\n\n**4.** Points arising from the last meeting are matters\n\n**5.** A formal decision recorded in minutes is a\n\n**6.** Repeating a structure three times for effect is\n\n**7.** Language including the audience with the speaker, such as *we* and *our*, is … language\n\n**8.** Write, in your own words, why minutes should record decisions rather than discussion.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Guided practice — A long formal letter with section headings**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.* Seven parts as before, but the body divided under ______ .\n\n- **Background** — two sentences at most.\n\n- **The problem** — with figures and ______ .\n\n- **What we ask for** — one paragraph, stated as an ______ .\n\n- **Contact** — a name and a telephone number. A letter nobody can answer will not be answered.\n\n> **Guided practice — Minutes in six parts**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\n- **Heading** — body, date, place, time.\n\n- **Present and apologies** — who came, and who sent ______ .\n\n- **Matters arising** — what happened to the last meeting's ______ points.\n\n- **Items** — one heading each, with the decision, not the ______ . **Any other business**, then the date of the next meeting. Sign and date the record.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Name the seven parts of a formal letter and say where section headings go.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Write a subject line and a first sentence for a letter asking the Assembly to repair a footbridge.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**3.** Explain what a copy line is and when it should be used.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** Write an agenda of six items for a school prefects' meeting.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**5.** Write minutes of one agenda item, ending with two action points naming who and by when.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**6.** Explain the difference between a resolution and an action point.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**7.** Write a letter to the editor of about eighty words on a local matter, with a claim and one piece of evidence.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n\n\n**8.** List the six things a flyer must contain, and design one for a school event.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**9.** Write an opening and a closing sentence for a speech at a school function.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**10.** Name four persuasive devices and give one example of each.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** Write a full formal letter of about two hundred and fifty words with four section headings, a subject line, a copy line and a contact.\n\n_[answer space — 10 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**2.** Write full minutes of a meeting of about two hundred words, in all six parts, with three action points.\n\n_[answer space — 14 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write a speech of about two hundred words for a school occasion, using tripling, one rhetorical question, parallel structure and inclusive language, and mark each.\n\n_[answer space — 9 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**4.** Explain the difference between persuading and manipulating, with one example of each.\n\n_[answer space — 5 lines]_\n\n**5.** Take a real flyer or poster and redesign it, listing every change and your reason.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n> **Writing task**\n\nChoose a real problem in your school or community and produce three documents about it: a formal letter of about two hundred and fifty words with a subject line, four section headings, a copy line and a contact; minutes of about a hundred and fifty words of a meeting held to discuss it, with three action points; and a flyer of not more than sixty words telling people what to do next. **Plan first**\n\n| **The problem, in one sentence** | **Who can act, and who should be copied** |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| **Three action points, each with a name and a date** | **What the flyer must make a reader do** |\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n**Now write.**\n\n_[answer space — 26 lines]_\n\n\n\n**Check your work**\n\n| **I have…** | **✓** |\n| --- | --- |\n| laid out the letter in all seven parts, with four section headings |  |\n| used a subject line, a copy line and a contact |  |\n| recorded decisions rather than discussion in the minutes |  |\n| gave every action point a name and a date |  |\n| made the flyer answer what, when, where, who and how to contact |  |\n\n> **Speaking and listening**\n\n*This task is carried out aloud. Your teacher or a partner signs it when you have done it.* Deliver your speech to the class in two minutes. Listeners name each persuasive device as they hear it, and say whether any was used to hide a weakness in the argument.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\nHold a full meeting with a chairperson and a secretary. The secretary produces minutes afterwards, and the meeting agrees them at the start of the next session.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Name the seven parts of a formal letter and say what a section heading adds to a long one. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**2.** Explain what a subject line and a copy line are, and when each is used. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n**3.** Name the six parts of minutes. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** Explain the difference between a resolution and an action point, with an example of each. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** Name four persuasive devices and give an example of two. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**6.** List the six things a flyer must contain. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Writing task | 10 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing · Sub-Strand: Building and Presenting Knowledge*"
